--- field object.service.name
type: str
asserted: true

The name of the service the event is about, as its definition names it.
The same value peinit passes as the object name in the KACS audit context
when it checks a caller's access to a service, so a peinit record and the
access-check record for the same command join on it.

On a `kacs.audit.access.checked` record the value is peinit's claim, copied
by the kernel from the check's audit context `{kind: "service", service:
{name}}`, so that record also carries `fields.attestation.userspace`.

A name, not a durable identity: a service removed and later redefined
under the same name is a different service with the same
`object.service.name`. Read `object.service.generation` beside it to tell
one activation from the next.

--- field object.service.type
type: str.enum
values: simple | oneshot
closed: false

How the service's main process is supervised. A `simple` service is up
while its process runs; a `oneshot` service runs to completion and is
then `completed`. Health checks are scheduled for `simple` services only,
which is why a validation finding names the type.

--- field object.service.state
type: str.enum
values: inactive | starting | active | reloading | stopping | completed | backoff | failed | abandoned | skipped
closed: true

The service's state after the event, in the vocabulary of the control
channel (PSPU §4.B). Exactly four states satisfy dependents: `active`,
`reloading`, `completed` and `skipped`. Use that set and no other when
asking whether something depending on this service could be running.

`abandoned` means a process survived SIGKILL and is still there,
unkillably. It is not a stopped service.

--- field object.service.state-previous
type: str.enum
values: inactive | starting | active | reloading | stopping | completed | backoff | failed | abandoned | skipped
closed: true

The service's state before the event, with the same values as
`object.service.state`. The two together are the transition; a record
carrying only one of them says where the service is, not how it got
there.

--- field object.service.transition-cause
type: str.enum
values: explicit-start | dependency-start | restart-policy | binds-to-recovery | timer | explicit-stop | explicit-reload | explicit-reset | conflict-eviction | binds-to-propagation | shutdown-wave | process-crash | clean-exit | clean-exit-restart | readiness-timeout | watchdog-timeout | health-check-failure | pre-hook-failure | parent-setup-failure | pre-exec-failure | dependency-failure | restart-budget-exhausted | cycle-detected | validation-error | assertion-error | condition-skipped | tty-unavailable | process-unkillable | internal-error
closed: false

Why the service's state changed. The causes beginning `explicit-` follow
an operator's command; the rest are peinit's own reasons, and an operator
reading one has learned what the manager did, not what anyone asked for.

**`internal-error` is a fault in peinit, not in the service.** The
service was failed because peinit could not carry out a step on its
behalf; the service itself may have been healthy.

--- field object.service.generation
type: uint

The service's activation generation: a counter peinit advances each time
the service enters `starting`, so that every incarnation of a service has
its own number. Records with the same `object.service.name` and the same
generation describe one run of the service.

The same counter as `object.job.activation-generation`, seen from the
service rather than from one of its jobs. It is held in memory and starts
again when peinit does, so it orders runs within one boot only.

--- field object.service.dependents
type: str[]

The services that hard-depend on this one, by name. Hard dependencies are
`requires` and `binds-to`; a `wants` dependent is not listed, because it
starts whether or not this service does.

--- field object.service.dependency.name
type: str

The name of the dependency a graph finding is about: the service this
one requires and cannot have, or the one whose block has blocked it.
Read `object.service.dependency.kind` for how this service depends on it.

The name is as the definition wrote it. A missing-dependency finding
names a service that does not exist, so do not expect to find any other
record about it.

--- field object.service.dependency.kind
type: str.enum
values: requires | wants | binds-to
closed: false

How the service depends on `object.service.dependency.name`. `requires`
and `binds-to` are hard: the service cannot start without the dependency.
`binds-to` is also tied to it afterwards, and stops and recovers with it.

--- field object.service.conflict.name
type: str

The name of the service this one conflicts with, on a finding that two
services both triggered at boot declare a conflict and so cannot both
start. Which of the pair is `object.service.name` and which is this field
carries no meaning: the conflict is mutual.

--- field object.service.on-failure.name
type: str

The OnFailure handler peinit declined to start, because starting it would
have looped or gone too deep. The failure the handler was meant to handle
went unhandled, so look for it here when a handler did not run.

--- field object.service.on-failure-chain
type: str[]

The OnFailure handlers already started in the chain that peinit cut
short, in the order they were started. A chain is cut when the next
handler is already in it, which is a loop, or when it has reached sixteen
handlers.

--- field object.service.failed
type: bool

Whether an internal error in peinit failed the service it occurred on.
False means peinit contained the error without changing the service's
state. Either way the fault was peinit's, not the service's.

--- field object.service.health-check.interval
type: uint.duration

The configured interval between health checks, in nanoseconds. Configured
in whole seconds, so it is always a whole number of seconds.

--- field object.service.health-check.retries
type: uint

The configured number of consecutive health-check failures at which
peinit stops reporting the service unhealthy and acts on it. Failures
short of this count change nothing but the service's health.

--- field object.service.health-check.restart-window
type: uint.duration

The configured restart window of a health check, in nanoseconds.
Configured in whole seconds, so it is always a whole number of seconds.
A validation finding carries it beside `object.service.health-check.interval`
and `object.service.health-check.retries`, because it is their
combination that a definition gets wrong.

--- field object.service.timer.schedule
type: str

The service's timer schedule, exactly as its definition wrote it. On a
finding that the schedule does not parse, this is the text that failed,
so it may not be a valid schedule at all.

--- field object.job.guid
type: bin.guid
asserted: true

The job the event is about. A job is one process peinit runs: a service's
main process, one of its hooks or health checks, or a job a client
submitted. The GUID is a UUIDv7, so it also orders jobs by when they were
created.

On a `kacs.audit.access.checked` record the value is peinit's claim, copied
by the kernel from the check's audit context `{kind: "job", job: {guid}}`,
so that record also carries `fields.attestation.userspace`.

--- field object.job.type
type: str.enum
values: service-main | pre-exec-hook | post-exec-hook | reload-hook | health-check | submitted
closed: true

What the job is for. Every type except `submitted` belongs to a service,
named in `object.service.name`; a `submitted` job belongs to the client
that submitted it, named in `object.job.submitter.sid`, and has no
service.

--- field object.job.state
type: str.enum
values: created | running | completed | failed | abandoned
closed: true

The job's state after the event. `completed`, `failed` and `abandoned`
are terminal. `abandoned` means its process survived SIGKILL: the job is
over but the process is still there.

--- field object.job.activation-generation
type: uint

The activation generation of the service the job belongs to, at the time
the job was created. The same counter as `object.service.generation`, so
it tells which run of a service a job served. Absent for a `submitted`
job, which belongs to no service.

--- field object.job.executable
type: str.path

The path of the executable the job runs.

--- field object.job.arguments
type: str[]

The job's argument vector, including `argv[0]`. Kept whole up to 32 KiB
of encoded arguments; past that, the arguments are cut at the last whole
argument that fits, never inside one, and `object.job.arguments-truncated`
is true. A cut argument vector is a prefix of the real one, so do not
read its last element as the last argument the job was given.

--- field object.job.arguments-count
type: uint

How many arguments the job was given, before any were cut. Equal to the
length of `object.job.arguments` unless that was cut.

--- field object.job.arguments-truncated
type: bool

Whether `object.job.arguments` was cut to fit the record. When true,
`object.job.arguments-count` says how many arguments there really were.

--- field object.job.created-time
type: uint.time

When peinit created the job, in nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.
Creation is when peinit decided to run it, before its process exists.

peinit times a job on the monotonic clock, and writes the instant as the
wall-clock time it was when it builds the record. If the wall clock was
stepped between the job's creation and its end, the time is reported on
the clock as it stood at the end.

--- field object.job.started-time
type: uint.time

When the job's process started, in nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.
Absent if the job never started, which is how a job that failed before
its process existed shows itself. Projected from the monotonic clock as
`object.job.created-time` is.

--- field object.job.duration
type: uint.duration

How long the job lasted, in nanoseconds, from its creation to its end.
**Measured from creation, not from start**, so it includes any time spent
waiting to start; subtract `object.job.created-time` from
`object.job.started-time` to find that part.

--- field object.job.submitter.sid
type: bin.sid

The user SID of the client that submitted the job. Present only on a
`submitted` job.

--- field object.job.token.sid
type: bin.sid

The user SID of the token the job's process runs as.

--- field object.job.token.groups
type: bin.sid[]

The group SIDs of the token the job's process runs as, in the token's
order.

--- field object.job.token.privileges
type: uint.flags
values: 0x4 KACS_SE_CREATE_TOKEN_PRIVILEGE | 0x8 KACS_SE_ASSIGN_PRIMARY_TOKEN_PRIVILEGE | 0x10 KACS_SE_LOCK_MEMORY_PRIVILEGE | 0x20 KACS_SE_INCREASE_QUOTA_PRIVILEGE | 0x80 KACS_SE_TCB_PRIVILEGE | 0x100 KACS_SE_SECURITY_PRIVILEGE | 0x200 KACS_SE_TAKE_OWNERSHIP_PRIVILEGE | 0x400 KACS_SE_LOAD_DRIVER_PRIVILEGE | 0x800 KACS_SE_SYSTEM_PROFILE_PRIVILEGE | 0x1000 KACS_SE_SYSTEMTIME_PRIVILEGE | 0x2000 KACS_SE_PROFILE_SINGLE_PROCESS_PRIVILEGE | 0x4000 KACS_SE_INCREASE_BASE_PRIORITY_PRIVILEGE | 0x20000 KACS_SE_BACKUP_PRIVILEGE | 0x40000 KACS_SE_RESTORE_PRIVILEGE | 0x80000 KACS_SE_SHUTDOWN_PRIVILEGE | 0x100000 KACS_SE_DEBUG_PRIVILEGE | 0x200000 KACS_SE_AUDIT_PRIVILEGE | 0x800000 KACS_SE_CHANGE_NOTIFY_PRIVILEGE | 0x1000000 KACS_SE_REMOTE_SHUTDOWN_PRIVILEGE | 0x10000000 KACS_SE_MANAGE_VOLUME_PRIVILEGE | 0x20000000 KACS_SE_IMPERSONATE_PRIVILEGE | 0x100000000 KACS_SE_RELABEL_PRIVILEGE | 0x800000000 KACS_SE_CREATE_SYMBOLIC_LINK_PRIVILEGE

The privileges present on the token the job's process runs as, as the
flags of the 64-bit privilege word of `uapi/pkm/token.h`, one
`KACS_SE_*_PRIVILEGE` bit each. Present is not enabled: read
`object.job.token.privileges-enabled` for which ones the process can use
without enabling them first.

Flags, as the kernel's `subject.token.privileges` are, not an access mask:
a `uint.mask` is decoded against `object.kind` (PGSS §6.5), which means
nothing on a job event.

--- field object.job.token.privileges-enabled
type: uint.flags
values: 0x4 KACS_SE_CREATE_TOKEN_PRIVILEGE | 0x8 KACS_SE_ASSIGN_PRIMARY_TOKEN_PRIVILEGE | 0x10 KACS_SE_LOCK_MEMORY_PRIVILEGE | 0x20 KACS_SE_INCREASE_QUOTA_PRIVILEGE | 0x80 KACS_SE_TCB_PRIVILEGE | 0x100 KACS_SE_SECURITY_PRIVILEGE | 0x200 KACS_SE_TAKE_OWNERSHIP_PRIVILEGE | 0x400 KACS_SE_LOAD_DRIVER_PRIVILEGE | 0x800 KACS_SE_SYSTEM_PROFILE_PRIVILEGE | 0x1000 KACS_SE_SYSTEMTIME_PRIVILEGE | 0x2000 KACS_SE_PROFILE_SINGLE_PROCESS_PRIVILEGE | 0x4000 KACS_SE_INCREASE_BASE_PRIORITY_PRIVILEGE | 0x20000 KACS_SE_BACKUP_PRIVILEGE | 0x40000 KACS_SE_RESTORE_PRIVILEGE | 0x80000 KACS_SE_SHUTDOWN_PRIVILEGE | 0x100000 KACS_SE_DEBUG_PRIVILEGE | 0x200000 KACS_SE_AUDIT_PRIVILEGE | 0x800000 KACS_SE_CHANGE_NOTIFY_PRIVILEGE | 0x1000000 KACS_SE_REMOTE_SHUTDOWN_PRIVILEGE | 0x10000000 KACS_SE_MANAGE_VOLUME_PRIVILEGE | 0x20000000 KACS_SE_IMPERSONATE_PRIVILEGE | 0x100000000 KACS_SE_RELABEL_PRIVILEGE | 0x800000000 KACS_SE_CREATE_SYMBOLIC_LINK_PRIVILEGE

Which of the privileges in `object.job.token.privileges` are enabled, as
flags in the same 64-bit word. Always a subset of it.

--- field object.cgroup.path
type: str.path

The cgroup the event is about, as its full path under
`/sys/fs/cgroup/peinit`. A service's cgroup is named for the service,
with any byte outside letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-` written as `%`
and two hexadecimal digits, so the path can differ from the service name.
Once a service's tree has leaked, later trees carry a `%gen` suffix and
the cgroup generation, so that a new run never shares a tree with an
unkillable process. A submitted job's cgroup is under `jobs/`, named by
the job's GUID.

--- field object.cgroup.type
type: str.enum
values: service-tree | health | hooks | helper
closed: false

Which part of a service's process containment the cgroup is.
`service-tree` is the whole of it and the most serious to lose; `health`
and `hooks` hold its health checks and hooks; `helper` is a pre-start
check helper's cgroup.

--- field object.cgroup.generation
type: uint

The cgroup generation of the service the cgroup belongs to. peinit
advances it when a service's tree is leaked, so that the next run gets a
fresh tree; it is the number in a path's `%gen` suffix. Not the same
counter as `object.service.generation`, which advances on every start.

--- field object.operation.guid
type: bin.guid

The peinit operation the event is about: a start, stop, restart, reload
or reset of one service, from the moment it is requested to the moment
it ends. The GUID is a UUIDv7.

**Not the generic `operation` domain.** `operation.name` and its
siblings describe what any event attempted; `object.operation` is a
thing peinit manages, and a search for one does not find the other.

--- field object.operation.type
type: str.enum
values: start | stop | restart | reload | reset
closed: true

What the operation does to its service.

--- field object.operation.state
type: str.enum
values: pending | running | completed | failed | merged | cancelled | aborted
closed: true

The operation's state after the event. `completed`, `failed`, `merged`,
`cancelled` and `aborted` are terminal. `cancelled` means it ended while
still pending and never ran; `aborted` means it ended while running;
`merged` means another operation absorbed it, named in
`object.operation.merged-into.guid`.

--- field object.operation.source
type: str.enum
values: admin | boot | shutdown | dependency-propagation | restart-policy | timer | binds-to-recovery | binds-to-propagation | conflict-resolution | on-failure | tty-release
closed: false

Who or what asked for the operation. `admin` is the only source a
client's command produces; every other source is peinit acting for its
own reasons. An `admin` operation is the one to tie to a person, through
the access check that admitted the command.

--- field object.operation.duration
type: uint.duration

How long the operation lasted, in nanoseconds, from when it was requested
to when it ended. **Measured from the request, not from when it started
running**, so it includes any time it spent pending behind other work.

--- field object.operation.merged-into.guid
type: bin.guid

The operation that absorbed this one. A merged operation's work is done,
or not, by the operation named here, so follow it to learn the outcome.

--- field subject.service.name
type: str

The service whose job sent a notification message. The sender is
authenticated by its process, so this is the service peinit established,
not one the message claimed.

--- field subject.job.guid
type: bin.guid

The job whose process sent a notification message: the service's main
process, or a submitted job.

--- field subject.job.activation-generation
type: uint

The activation generation of the service whose job sent a notification
message, as `object.job.activation-generation` describes it. A message
from an earlier run of a service carries an older generation.

--- field subject.operation.guid
type: bin.guid

The peinit operation the sending job was serving when it sent a
notification message, such as the start that a readiness message
completes. Absent when the job serves none.

--- field notify.status
type: str

The text a service sent as `STATUS=`: free text describing what it is
doing. The service's own words, unvalidated; never read it as a
statement by peinit.

--- field notify.errno
type: int.errno

The error number a service sent as `ERRNO=`, as a negative errno. Services
send it positive, by convention; this field holds it negated.

The field is the service's claim about itself, informational only; peinit
neither acts on nor retains it. The protocol does not require the text to
be a number: text that is not a positive number has no errno to carry, and
the record is written without this field.

--- field notify.exit-status
type: int

The exit status a service sent as `EXIT_STATUS=`, informationally. Not
the exit code of any process: a service may send it while still running,
and its real exit appears in `object.process.exit-code`. Text that is not
an integer is not carried, and the record is written without this field.

--- field notify.progress.current
type: uint

How far a service or submitted job has got, as it reported with
`PROGRESS=`: the count before any `/`. Its unit is
`notify.progress.unit`. It may fall from one record to the next; do not
read motion from the difference between two.

--- field notify.progress.total
type: uint

The end `notify.progress.current` is counting towards, as reported after
the `/` of `PROGRESS=`. At least 1, and never less than the current
value. The total may change between records, because a job that
rescans has learned something.

Absent when the service gave no total, and **absence alone does not say
whether one is coming**. `PROGRESS=N` counts with no end, while
`PROGRESS=N/` counts towards an end not yet known, and both leave this
field absent; `notify.progress.bounded` is what tells them apart.

--- field notify.progress.bounded
type: bool

Whether the progress a service or submitted job reported declares an end:
true for `PROGRESS=N/` and `PROGRESS=N/M`, false for a bare `PROGRESS=N`,
which counts with no end (PSPU §4.19). It is what tells a viewer to draw a
bar or to expect one rather than show a rising count.

True with `notify.progress.total` absent means the end is declared but not
yet known. Absent when no progress figure has been reported, as when a
service has sent only `PROGRESS_UNIT=`.

Not implied by `notify.progress.total`: `PROGRESS=N` and `PROGRESS=N/` both
leave the total absent, and PSPU §4.19 has a viewer draw them differently.

--- field notify.progress.unit
type: str.enum
values: bytes | items | percent
closed: true

What `notify.progress.current` and `notify.progress.total` count, as
reported with `PROGRESS_UNIT=`. Absent when the service gave no unit.

--- field graph.context
type: uint

The graph execution context an event belongs to: one boot's start of its
service set, or one on-demand start of a service and everything it needs.
Records with the same context belong to one attempt to bring a set of
services up.

Numbered from 0 in the order peinit creates contexts. Not durable: the
numbering starts again when peinit does.

--- field graph.phase
type: str.enum
values: boot | reload-config | phase2-boot
closed: false

When the service graph was validated. The phase changes what a finding
did: at `boot`, the services it names are marked and the boot continues;
at `reload-config`, the whole reload is rejected and nothing is applied;
at `phase2-boot`, the boot plan itself could not be built.

--- field graph.services
type: str[]

The services a graph finding involves. For a dependency cycle, the
services in the cycle, in the order the cycle runs; for an unfilled role, the
services that need the role and cannot start; for a reload during the
boot window, the services whose definitions were deferred.

--- field graph.role
type: str

The role no service fills. A role is a virtual name a service declares it
provides, such as `authn`, and other services depend on the role rather
than on a service name. The services that need it are in
`graph.services`.

--- field graph.counts.added
type: uint

How many service definitions a configuration reload added: names that
had no definition before.

Written only when the reload succeeded. A failed reload changed nothing,
and carries no counts.

--- field graph.counts.updated
type: uint

How many existing service definitions a configuration reload changed.
Written only when the reload succeeded, as `graph.counts.added` is.

--- field graph.counts.restored
type: uint

How many definitions a configuration reload brought back after an
earlier reload had removed them while their services were still
running. Written only when the reload succeeded, as
`graph.counts.added` is.

--- field graph.counts.marked-removed
type: uint

How many definitions a configuration reload withdrew from services that
were still running. Such a service is left running, cannot be started
again, and is discarded once it stops. Written only when the reload
succeeded, as `graph.counts.added` is.

--- field graph.counts.discarded
type: uint

How many definitions a configuration reload withdrew and dropped at
once, because their services were not running. Written only when the
reload succeeded, as `graph.counts.added` is.

--- field graph.counts.undecodable
type: uint

How many definitions a configuration reload found in the registry but
could not decode. Each such service is failed, or, if it was running,
left running with its definition marked removed. Not counted under
`graph.counts.marked-removed` or `graph.counts.discarded`, because the
key is still there. Written only when the reload succeeded, as
`graph.counts.added` is.

--- field object.fd-store.name
type: str

The name a service gave a file descriptor it asked peinit to keep, with
`FDNAME=`. A descriptor sent without a name is stored as `stored`.
Names are not unique: a service may store several descriptors under one
name, and two services may use the same one, so a name identifies
nothing on its own without `object.service.name`.

--- field shutdown.finalization-state
type: str.enum
values: waiting-for-services | ready | failed | completed
closed: false

How far the system shutdown had got when the event was written.
`waiting-for-services` means services were still being stopped; `ready`
means they had stopped and the final action had not yet run; `failed`
means finalisation itself went wrong; `completed` means it finished.

--- event peinit.job.created
tier: verbose
gating: none — every job peinit creates is recorded

A job exists: peinit has decided to run a process and recorded it, before
the process exists. The gap to `peinit.job.started` is time spent queued,
and `peinit.job.ended` repeats everything here, which is why this one is
verbose.

Formerly `job.created`.

field: object.job.guid                      required
field: object.job.type                      required
field: object.job.state                     required
  Always `created`.
field: object.service.name                  when object.job.type != submitted
field: object.job.activation-generation     when object.job.type != submitted
field: object.operation.guid                optional
  The operation the job serves. Absent when none asked for it, as for a
  submitted job.
field: object.job.executable                required
field: object.job.token.sid                 required
field: object.job.token.groups              required
field: object.job.token.privileges          required
field: object.job.token.privileges-enabled  required

--- event peinit.job.started
tier: standard
gating: none — every job whose process starts is recorded

A job's process exists and has been executed: the moment a process is
running as `object.job.token.sid` on peinit's behalf.

Formerly `job.started`.

field: object.job.guid                      required
field: object.job.type                      required
field: object.job.state                     required
  Always `running`.
field: object.service.name                  when object.job.type != submitted
field: object.job.activation-generation     when object.job.type != submitted
field: object.operation.guid                optional
field: object.process.pid                   required
  The job's process.
field: object.cgroup.path                   required
  The cgroup the job's process runs in.
field: object.job.token.sid                 required
field: object.job.token.groups              required
field: object.job.token.privileges          required
field: object.job.token.privileges-enabled  required

--- event peinit.job.ended
tier: standard
gating: none — every job that ends is recorded

A job is over: its process exited or was killed, or the job ended without
its process ever starting. It carries the whole job record, so it is the
one job event to read when only one is kept.

Formerly `job.ended`.

field: object.job.guid                      required
field: object.job.type                      required
field: object.job.state                     required
  The terminal state: `completed`, `failed` or `abandoned`.
field: outcome.success                      required
  True exactly when `object.job.state` is `completed`.
field: outcome.detail                       optional
  Why the job did not complete, in peinit's words, such as
  `ProcessCrash: signal 9`. Present only when `outcome.success` is false
  and peinit recorded a cause. Free text until it is given an enumeration.
field: object.service.name                  when object.job.type != submitted
field: object.job.activation-generation     when object.job.type != submitted
field: object.operation.guid                optional
field: object.process.pid                   optional
  Absent when the process never started.
field: object.process.exit-code             optional
field: object.process.exit-signal           optional
field: object.job.executable                required
field: object.job.arguments                 required
field: object.job.arguments-truncated       required
field: object.job.arguments-count           required
field: object.job.created-time              required
field: object.job.started-time              optional
field: object.job.duration                  required
field: object.cgroup.path                   required
field: object.cgroup.generation             required
field: object.job.token.sid                 required
field: object.job.token.groups              required
field: object.job.token.privileges          required
field: object.job.token.privileges-enabled  required

--- event peinit.job.status.reported
tier: verbose
gating: none — every change a submitted job reports is recorded, within the rate bound
cardinality: at most one per job per second; a report inside the window updates the job view and writes nothing

A submitted job reported `STATUS=` or `PROGRESS=` on the notification
channel, and this is what peinit retained after it.

Formerly `job.status`.

field: object.job.guid                      required
field: object.job.submitter.sid             required
field: notify.status                        optional
field: notify.progress.current              optional
field: notify.progress.total                optional
field: notify.progress.bounded              optional
field: notify.progress.unit                 optional

--- event peinit.job.output.dropped
tier: standard
gating: none
cardinality: once per job, on the first line dropped

A submitted job's submitter stopped draining its output, and peinit began
dropping the submitter's copy of the job's lines. The copy sent to the log
store is unaffected.

Formerly `output.dropped`.

field: object.job.guid                      required

--- event peinit.operation.requested
tier: standard
gating: none — every operation is recorded

An operation on a service — a start, stop, restart, reload or reset — was
created. A client's command creates one with `object.operation.source`
`admin`; every other source is peinit acting for its own reasons. Every
operation in a graph context is requested when the context is built.

Formerly `operation.requested`.

field: object.operation.guid                required
field: object.operation.type                required
field: object.operation.source              required
field: object.operation.state               required
  Always `pending`.
field: object.service.name                  required
field: subject.token.sid                    optional
  The client whose command created the operation. Absent for an operation
  peinit created itself.
field: subject.token.groups                 optional
  Written only when peinit holds the client's whole token. For a
  control-channel caller it holds the user SID alone, so today an
  operation's subject is `subject.token.sid` and nothing more.
field: subject.token.privileges             optional
  Present exactly when `subject.token.groups` is.
field: subject.token.privileges-enabled     optional
  Present exactly when `subject.token.groups` is.

--- event peinit.operation.started
tier: verbose
gating: none — every operation that begins to run is recorded

An operation left the queue and began to run.

Formerly `operation.started`.

field: object.operation.guid                required
field: object.operation.type                required
field: object.operation.source              required
field: object.operation.state               required
  Always `running`.
field: object.service.name                  required
field: subject.token.sid                    optional
field: subject.token.groups                 optional
field: subject.token.privileges             optional
field: subject.token.privileges-enabled     optional

--- event peinit.operation.ended
tier: standard
gating: none — every operation that ends, other than by merging, is recorded

An operation reached a terminal state other than `merged`: it completed,
failed, was cancelled while still pending, or was aborted while running.
One type for the four, because they are outcomes of one action (PGSS
§6.6); `object.operation.state` says which.

Formerly `operation.completed`, `operation.failed`, `operation.cancelled`
and `operation.aborted`.

field: object.operation.guid                required
field: object.operation.type                required
field: object.operation.source              required
field: object.operation.state               required
  `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` or `aborted`.
field: outcome.success                      required
  True exactly when `object.operation.state` is `completed`.
field: outcome.detail                       optional
  The operation's result as peinit words it for a client, such as
  `inactive` or `startup killed by shutdown`. Free text until it is given
  an enumeration.
field: object.operation.duration            optional
  From the request, not from the start. Absent on a cancelled operation,
  which never ran.
field: object.service.name                  required
field: subject.token.sid                    optional
field: subject.token.groups                 optional
field: subject.token.privileges             optional
field: subject.token.privileges-enabled     optional

--- event peinit.operation.merged
tier: verbose
gating: none — every merge is recorded

An operation was absorbed into another before it ran, and has no outcome
of its own: follow `object.operation.merged-into.guid` to the operation
whose `peinit.operation.ended` decides it.

Formerly `operation.merged`.

field: object.operation.guid                required
field: object.operation.type                required
field: object.operation.source              required
field: object.operation.state               required
  Always `merged`.
field: object.operation.merged-into.guid    required
field: object.service.name                  required
field: subject.token.sid                    optional
field: subject.token.groups                 optional
field: subject.token.privileges             optional
field: subject.token.privileges-enabled     optional

--- event peinit.graph.operation.ended
tier: verbose
gating: none — every member of a graph context is recorded

An operation that is a member of a graph execution context reached its
terminal outcome as the graph sees it: satisfied, so that what depends on
it may proceed, or failed. The operation's own `peinit.operation.ended`
records the same end from the operation's side.

Formerly `graph.operation_terminal`.

field: graph.context                        required
field: object.operation.guid                required
field: object.service.name                  required
field: outcome.success                      required
  True when the graph counts the member satisfied, false when it failed.

--- event peinit.graph.validation.failed
tier: standard
gating: none — every finding is recorded
cardinality: one per finding; a validation with several findings writes several

The service graph failed validation for the reason in `outcome.reason`. At
`boot` the services named are marked and the boot continues; at
`reload-config` the whole reload is rejected; at `phase2-boot` the boot
plan could not be built.

Formerly `graph.validation_error`.

field: graph.phase                          required
field: outcome.reason                       required
  values: invalid-service-name | duplicate-service | missing-service-definition | missing-hard-dependency | hard-dependency-blocked | cycle | conflicting-boot-services | invalid-health-check-restart-window | unschedulable-health-check | invalid-timer-schedule | validation-error
  closed: false
  The finding.
field: object.service.name                  optional
  The service the finding is about. Absent on `cycle`, which names several.
field: graph.services                       when outcome.reason == cycle
field: object.service.dependency.name       optional
  On `missing-hard-dependency` and `hard-dependency-blocked`.
field: object.service.dependency.kind       optional
  Present exactly when `object.service.dependency.name` is.
field: object.service.conflict.name         when outcome.reason == conflicting-boot-services
field: object.service.health-check.interval when outcome.reason == invalid-health-check-restart-window
field: object.service.health-check.retries  when outcome.reason == invalid-health-check-restart-window
field: object.service.health-check.restart-window when outcome.reason == invalid-health-check-restart-window
field: object.service.type                  when outcome.reason == unschedulable-health-check
field: object.service.timer.schedule        when outcome.reason == invalid-timer-schedule
field: outcome.detail                       optional
  On `invalid-timer-schedule`, the parser's message; on `validation-error`,
  why the definition was refused.

--- event peinit.graph.validation.warned
tier: standard
gating: none — every warning is recorded
cardinality: one per warning

The service graph passed validation with a warning: nothing was refused,
but something will not behave as its author probably expects.

Formerly `graph.validation_warning`.

field: graph.phase                          required
field: outcome.reason                       required
  values: alive-readiness-with-hard-dependents | unfilled-role
  closed: false
  The warning.
field: object.service.name                  when outcome.reason == alive-readiness-with-hard-dependents
field: object.service.dependents            when outcome.reason == alive-readiness-with-hard-dependents
field: graph.role                           when outcome.reason == unfilled-role
field: graph.services                       when outcome.reason == unfilled-role

--- event peinit.boot.downgraded
tier: essential
gating: none
cardinality: one per finding that forced the downgrade, at most once a boot in all

A full boot was downgraded to safe mode: the machine is running a reduced
service set. The services named are not marked failed, because safe mode
never meant to start them, so this record is the only one that names them
as the cause. Essential because it is rare and changes what the whole
machine is running.

Formerly `boot.safe_mode_downgrade`.

field: outcome.reason                       required
  values: critical-cycle | critical-boot-conflict
  closed: false
  The finding that forced the downgrade.
field: graph.services                       when outcome.reason == critical-cycle
field: object.service.name                  when outcome.reason == critical-boot-conflict
field: object.service.conflict.name         when outcome.reason == critical-boot-conflict

--- event peinit.recovery.entered
tier: essential
gating: none
cardinality: once, when peinit gives up on the boot and starts the recovery shell

peinit dropped the machine to a recovery shell, for the reason in
`outcome.reason`. Written before the registry or eventd may exist, into the
ring, which is where the record of a boot that failed this early survives.
Essential because it is rare and means the machine did not boot.

Formerly `recovery.entered`.

field: outcome.reason                       required
  values: kernel-command-line | privileges | boot-attempt-counter | forced-by-kernel-command-line | boot-attempt-threshold-reached | root-writable | virtual-filesystems | machine-id | rtc-clock | registryd | provisioning | infrastructure | phase2 | runtime
  closed: false
field: outcome.detail                       optional
  What went wrong, in the error's own words, such as `peinit is missing
  required privilege(s): SeAuditPrivilege`. Absent for
  `forced-by-kernel-command-line`, which has nothing to add, and for
  `phase2`, whose findings are their own `peinit.graph.validation.failed`
  records.

--- event peinit.critical-service.failed
tier: essential
gating: none
cardinality: once per Critical failure that drives the machine to reboot

A service marked Critical failed, and peinit took the machine to its
reboot final action. Essential because it is rare and reboots the machine.

Formerly `critical.failure`.

field: object.service.name                  required
field: outcome.reason                       required
  values: service-main-terminal | health-check-failure | watchdog-timeout | restart-budget-exhausted
  closed: false
  How the Critical service failed.
field: shutdown.finalization-state          required
field: outcome.detail                       when shutdown.finalization-state == failed
  Why finalisation failed.

--- event peinit.service.abandoned
tier: standard
gating: none
cardinality: once per service abandoned

A service's processes survived SIGKILL and its post-kill timeout, so peinit
gave up on them and moved the service to `abandoned`: its cgroup is still
populated, unkillably. Written only on the shutdown path so far; the
name also covers an abandonment anywhere else.

Formerly `shutdown.abandoned`.

field: object.service.name                  required
field: object.cgroup.path                   required
field: object.service.state-previous        required
field: object.service.state                 required
  Always `abandoned`.
field: object.service.transition-cause      required
field: object.service.generation            required

--- event peinit.cgroup.leaked
tier: standard
gating: none
cardinality: once per cgroup, the first time it is found still populated after its post-kill deadline

A cgroup could not be reclaimed: its processes do not respond to the
kernel, and the cgroup is leaked. It is one of a service's sub-cgroups, or
the whole cgroup of a submitted job.

Formerly `cgroup.leaked`.

field: object.service.name                  optional
  The service whose cgroup leaked. Absent for a submitted job's cgroup,
  which belongs to no service.
field: object.job.guid                      optional
  The submitted job whose cgroup leaked. Exactly one of
  `object.service.name` and this field is present.
field: object.cgroup.path                   required
field: object.cgroup.type                   required
  `service-tree` for a submitted job's cgroup, which is the whole of its
  containment.

--- event peinit.on-failure.suppressed
tier: standard
gating: none — every suppressed handler is recorded

peinit declined to start an OnFailure handler, because starting it would
have looped or gone too deep, so the failure it was meant to handle went
unhandled.

Formerly `on_failure.loop_suppressed`.

field: object.service.name                  required
  The service whose failure the chain was handling.
field: object.service.on-failure.name       required
field: object.service.on-failure-chain      required
field: outcome.reason                       required
  values: cycle | max-depth
  closed: true

--- event peinit.service.reload.timed-out
tier: standard
gating: none — every reload left unconfirmed past its deadline is recorded

A service announced `RELOADING=1` and never confirmed the reload by its
deadline: it wedged mid-reload, or lost its handler. Recorded because the
default `reload` does not wait, so nothing else would say so.

Formerly `service.reload_unconfirmed`.

field: object.service.name                  required

--- event peinit.internal-error.contained
tier: standard
gating: none — every contained internal error is recorded

peinit could not carry out a step on a service's behalf, and contained the
fault to that service. The fault is peinit's, not the service's; the job,
operation and state change it caused have their own events.

The error itself is not carried: every path that contains one has it only
as Rust Debug output, which no event field admits. peinit's console line
and the failed operation's result keep it.

Formerly `service.internal_error`.

field: operation.stage                      required
  values: job-terminal | process-setup | lifecycle-deadline | notify
  closed: false
  The step peinit could not carry out.
field: object.service.name                  optional
field: object.job.guid                      optional
field: object.service.failed                optional
  Present exactly when `object.service.name` is.

--- event peinit.event.dropped
tier: essential
gating: none
cardinality: once per event the ring refused

The ring refused one of peinit's events, and it is gone. Written so that
the trail records the gap rather than, for example, a job with a
`peinit.job.started` and no `peinit.job.ended`. Small by construction, and
if the ring refuses this one too peinit treats it as fatal. Essential
because it is the record of a hole in the audit trail.

There is no count of drops on the record: count the records. The
`truncated` notice that used to follow a cut `job.ended` is gone, because
`peinit.job.ended` says itself when its arguments were cut.

Formerly `event.oversized`, with action `dropped`.

field: emission.type                        required
field: emission.payload-length              required
field: object.service.name                  optional
  The service the refused event was about, or whose job sent it.
field: object.job.guid                      optional
  The job the refused event was about, or whose process sent it.
field: outcome.errno                        optional
  The error the emit returned, where it gave a number.

--- event peinit.config.reload.deferred
tier: verbose
gating: none
cardinality: once per reload during the boot window that left definitions pending

A configuration reload during the boot window changed definitions of
services the boot plan had not yet launched. The boot runs against its
snapshot, so those services start from the plan, and the change waits for
`peinit.config.reload.applied`.

Formerly `config.reload_deferred`.

field: graph.services                       required
  The services whose definitions were deferred.

--- event peinit.config.reload.applied
tier: standard
gating: none
cardinality: once per explicit `reload-config`, and once a boot at most when the boot window closes with reloads deferred

A configuration reload ran: from now on the registry as it then stood is
the running configuration, or, if it failed, nothing changed. Written for
an explicit `reload-config`, and for the one reload that applies what the
boot window deferred. A reload a registry watch starts writes none.

A failed validation also writes one `peinit.graph.validation.failed` per
finding, under the `reload-config` phase.

Formerly `config.reload_coalesced`, which an explicit reload did not write.

field: outcome.success                      required
field: outcome.detail                       optional
  Why the reload failed, for a person. Present only when
  `outcome.success` is false.
field: graph.services                       optional
  The services whose definitions the boot window had deferred. Present
  exactly on the reload that applies them, which is how it is told from an
  explicit `reload-config`.
field: graph.counts.added                   when outcome.success == true
field: graph.counts.updated                 when outcome.success == true
field: graph.counts.restored                when outcome.success == true
field: graph.counts.marked-removed          when outcome.success == true
field: graph.counts.discarded               when outcome.success == true
field: graph.counts.undecodable             when outcome.success == true

--- event peinit.notify.status.reported
tier: verbose
gating: none — every authenticated `STATUS=` is recorded

A service's job sent `STATUS=` on the notification channel. The text is the
service's own words, not peinit's. Not rate-bounded: a service decides how
often it sends one.

Formerly `notify.status`.

field: subject.service.name                 required
field: subject.job.guid                     required
field: subject.job.activation-generation    required
field: subject.operation.guid               optional
field: notify.status                        required

--- event peinit.notify.errno.reported
tier: standard
gating: none — every authenticated `ERRNO=` is recorded

A service's job sent `ERRNO=`: its own claim about an error, which peinit
neither acts on nor retains.

Formerly `notify.errno`.

field: subject.service.name                 required
field: subject.job.guid                     required
field: subject.job.activation-generation    required
field: subject.operation.guid               optional
field: notify.errno                         optional
  Absent when the text sent was not a positive number.

--- event peinit.notify.exit-status.reported
tier: standard
gating: none — every authenticated `EXIT_STATUS=` is recorded

A service's job sent `EXIT_STATUS=`, informationally; it is not the exit
of any process.

Formerly `notify.exit_status`.

field: subject.service.name                 required
field: subject.job.guid                     required
field: subject.job.activation-generation    required
field: subject.operation.guid               optional
field: notify.exit-status                   optional
  Absent when the text sent was not a number.

--- event peinit.notify.stopping.reported
tier: standard
gating: none — every authenticated `STOPPING=1` is recorded

A service's job sent `STOPPING=1`, so peinit will not send it SIGTERM. The
record exists because the effect is an absence: without it, a service that
rightly got no SIGTERM looks the same afterwards as one that wrongly did
not.

Formerly `notify.stopping`.

field: subject.service.name                 required
field: subject.job.guid                     required
field: subject.job.activation-generation    required
field: subject.operation.guid               optional

--- event peinit.notify.progress.reported
tier: verbose
gating: none — every change of progress is recorded, within the rate bound
cardinality: at most one per service activation per second

A service's job sent `PROGRESS=` or `PROGRESS_UNIT=`, and this is the
progress peinit retained after it.

Formerly `notify.progress`.

field: subject.service.name                 required
field: subject.job.guid                     required
field: subject.job.activation-generation    required
field: subject.operation.guid               optional
field: notify.progress.current              optional
field: notify.progress.total                optional
field: notify.progress.bounded              optional
field: notify.progress.unit                 optional

--- event peinit.notify.rejected
tier: standard
gating: none — every refused notification datagram is recorded

peinit refused a datagram on the notification channel and applied none of
it: the sender could not be authenticated, the message did not parse, or
it made no sense for the job's state. A stream of these from one process
is worth reading.

Formerly `notify.rejected`.

field: outcome.reason                       required
  values: invalid-utf8 | malformed-line | truncated | unauthenticated-sender | missing-service | job-not-running | missing-process | pidfd-mismatch | process-verification-failed | generation-mismatch | missing-start-operation | missing-reload-operation | unsupported-reload-operation | unsupported-ready-state | shutdown-refused | internal-error
  closed: false
  Why the datagram was refused. `internal-error` is peinit unable to act on
  it, not anything wrong with the datagram; `shutdown-refused` is a
  timeout extension (`EXTEND_TIMEOUT_USEC=`) the shutdown in progress
  would not grant.
field: subject.process.pid                  optional
  The sending process, when the kernel gave its credentials.
field: subject.service.name                 optional
  The sender's service, when peinit could attribute it.
field: subject.job.guid                     optional
field: subject.job.activation-generation    optional
field: subject.operation.guid               optional

--- event peinit.fd-store.rejected
tier: standard
gating: none — every refused descriptor is recorded

peinit refused a file descriptor a service asked it to keep.

Formerly `fd_store.rejected`.

field: object.service.name                  required
field: object.fd-store.name                 required
field: outcome.reason                       required
  values: disabled | full
  closed: false
  `disabled` when the service's definition does not enable an fd store;
  `full` when it is at its limit.
